```sas
ods pdf file="output.pdf" style=journal;
proc print data=sashelp.cars;
  title "SAS Journal Output";
  footnote "This is a footnote";
  id type;
  var make model msrp;
run;
ods pdf close;
```

In this example:

* The `ods pdf` statement specifies the output file name and style.
* The `style=journal` parameter specifies the journal style.
* The `proc print` statement is used to print the data to the PDF file.
* The `title`, `footnote`, `id`, and `var` statements are used to customize the output.

You can customize the journal style by modifying the `ods graphics` statement. For example:

```sas
ods graphics on;
ods pdf file="output.pdf" style=journal;
proc print data=sashelp.cars;
  title "SAS Journal Output";
  footnote "This is a footnote";
  id type;
  var make model msrp;
run;
ods pdf close;
ods graphics off;
```

In this example, the `ods graphics on` statement turns on graphics output, and the `ods graphics off` statement turns it back off. This allows you to use graphics statements, such as `ods graphics linegroup`, to customize the appearance of the journal.

