Here are the modifications you can make:

** Modify the existing `location /` block to include a link tag **

Add the following code inside the `location /` block:
```html
<link rel="canonical" href="https://www.askfundu.com{{$uri}}/" />
```
This will add a canonical link tag to each HTML page that points to the same URL but with the `www` subdomain.

** Add a new location block for generating links **

Add the following code after the `location /` block:
```nginx
location ~ (.*) {
 set $canonical_url "https://www.askfundu.com$1";
 rewrite ^(.*)$ $canonical_url permanent;
}
```
This block will rewrite every request to add the `www` subdomain to the URL.

** Test and deploy the changes **

After making the changes, test your Nginx configuration by restarting the Nginx service and verifying that the links are being generated correctly. If everything looks good, deploy the changes to your production environment.

Here's the modified Nginx configuration:
```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    listen [::]:443 ssl http2;
    server_name www.askfundu.com;
    root /usr/share/nginx/html;

    # ... (other config)

    location / {
        root /usr/share/nginx/html;
        index index.html index.htm;
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
        <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.askfundu.com{{$uri}}/" />
    }

    location ~ (.*) {
        set $canonical_url "https://www.askfundu.com$1";
        rewrite ^(.*)$ $canonical_url permanent;
    }

    # ... (other config)
}
```
